00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:36.320
- We had so much
- [Gabriela] We-

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:37.153
- Yeah, go ahead.
- [Gabriela] No, no you go.

00:01:37.153 --> 00:01:39.110
- We had so much fun making the show and,

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.629
all of us, secret is that
we're super competitive, right?

00:01:42.629 --> 00:01:43.462
- (Kelly) Yeah.

00:01:43.462 --> 00:01:44.295
- I love how Curtis said like,

00:01:44.295 --> 00:01:45.203
"It's not about winning and losing,"

00:01:45.203 --> 00:01:46.481
It is about winning and losing, though!

00:01:46.481 --> 00:01:47.631
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:47.631 --> 00:01:48.464
No, no!

00:01:48.464 --> 00:01:49.369
- He's low key about it!

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:52.279
He's like, hey, being competitive is fun.

00:01:52.279 --> 00:01:54.070
- [Marcus] Yes!
- It's fun.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.332
- But the other thing I
love about this Iron Chef

00:01:56.332 --> 00:01:59.780
is the talent of these young cooks now,

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.040
in this country, are phenomenal.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.110
And beyond this country.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.230
So there's that.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:07.460
The stage, the technology,
the amazing work

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:08.910
they put into kitchen stadium,

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:09.860
it's unbelievable.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.420
Mark Dacascos is back, right?

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.070
The chairman is back and
Alton Brown, which is awesome.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:14.903
- [Marcus] Yeah, the icon.

00:02:14.903 --> 00:02:16.590
- Right? We, they added Christian Kish

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.366
Who was a fantastic chef and
she's a fantastic commentator.

00:02:20.366 --> 00:02:22.769
And it's about the Iron Legend

00:02:22.769 --> 00:02:24.798
Which has never been done before.

00:02:24.798 --> 00:02:25.870
Right?

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:29.420
So someone that wins that
ends up beating one of us

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:30.987
can then compete against all of us.

00:02:30.987 --> 00:02:34.640
And if they beat us, they
become the first Iron Legend.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.566
It's never been done before.

00:02:35.566 --> 00:02:38.038
- I just like how dramatic that sounds

00:02:38.038 --> 00:02:40.140
like Quest for an Iron Legend,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.700
I feel like I'm Zelda.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:42.958
Like I feel like I'm playing
that game when I was a kid.

00:02:42.958 --> 00:02:45.010
Cause I love the quest part of it.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.080
It's all awesome.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.308
But Dominique and Gabriela,

00:02:47.308 --> 00:02:50.670
Were you all fans of the
older "Iron Chef" version?

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:54.270
- I was very much a fan
of the "Japanese Iron Chef".

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:55.980
- Yeah.
- Yeah, it was pretty amazing.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.985
- But I was looking of that in,

00:02:58.985 --> 00:03:01.680
I think 2009 or 2010.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.910
I did the "Iron Chef America"
against Michael Symon.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:06.743
- [Marcus] How did you do?

00:03:06.743 --> 00:03:08.193
- Oh, kicked his ass.

00:03:08.193 --> 00:03:10.642
(audience laughs)

00:03:10.642 --> 00:03:12.559
- Oh my god. Oh my god.

00:03:13.713 --> 00:03:14.546
Oh my god.

00:03:15.443 --> 00:03:16.276
I love it.

00:03:16.276 --> 00:03:18.094
- If I could add, I was also on "Iron Chef",

00:03:18.094 --> 00:03:19.150
- [Gabriela] It's good that when

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.360
- I was also on "Iron Chef America"

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:23.142
against Bobby Flay, and
his ass also got kicked.

00:03:23.142 --> 00:03:25.947
- I love it! Oh my god, I

00:03:25.947 --> 00:03:26.780
- It's good that we're not competitive.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:27.690
It's good that we're not competitive.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.030
- I know, I love that this started out

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.370
It was like, we're all
just really here to like,

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:33.480
bless each other with our experiences.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:34.920
And then it like gets cutthroat.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.310
I love it.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.490
That's why people love it too though,

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:38.394
It's so fun.

00:03:38.394 --> 00:03:40.990
So Ming, you're all passionate about-

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:42.370
Or all of you are passionate about

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.470
supporting the restaurant industry.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.779
So how can people help?

00:03:44.779 --> 00:03:46.290
- Ah, that's a great question.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:47.123
Thanks Kelly.

00:03:47.123 --> 00:03:47.956
- (Kelly) Yeah.

00:03:47.956 --> 00:03:50.080
- You know, you just gotta
come to our restaurants, right?

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.020
Go out and support and be kind, right?

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:55.739
There's some, some people
are going to restaurants

00:03:55.739 --> 00:03:57.137
and they're like impatient
and yelling, these people

00:03:57.137 --> 00:03:58.700
- Those are people that have never been

00:03:58.700 --> 00:03:59.720
in the service industry.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.260
- Exactly. So be kind,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.960
because everyone is
breaking their backside

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:03.881
to get food to you.

00:04:03.881 --> 00:04:04.714
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:04.714 --> 00:04:06.090
- And tip large, right?

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:07.702
I mean, if you can afford it, tip large.

00:04:07.702 --> 00:04:09.070
- That's so true though.

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:10.291
Especially the last two years.

00:04:10.291 --> 00:04:11.250
- This is the best way to
support the restaurants.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.030
Go out, go and big groups, tip
large and just keep it going.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:15.840
Cause that energy it's what,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.251
it's why we're in the
hospitality industry.

00:04:18.251 --> 00:04:19.084
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:19.084 --> 00:04:20.251
- Right? We want to serve

00:04:20.251 --> 00:04:21.322
We wanna make people happy
through food and service.

00:04:21.322 --> 00:04:25.290
- And I think people realize
like how great we had it.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:26.672
Like before two years ago,
we realized how great,

00:04:26.672 --> 00:04:28.000
you could go into a restaurant

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:28.990
and you had these amazing
meals cooked for you

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:30.633
that you could never cook at home.

00:04:30.633 --> 00:04:32.440
And if you say you can, you're lying

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:33.580
and you're not that good.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:36.340
And I'm just saying like,
it's an amazing experience

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:38.410
to be able to go into someone's
house where they're cooking

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:39.690
- But it's also restaurants
are part of every neighborhood

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.487
It's really the heartbeat of
your community, no matter what.

00:04:42.487 --> 00:04:43.550
- And it's where you gather.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:44.570
- Yeah, it's where you gather,

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.700
If you can't just visit, do something

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:48.620
because the neighborhood,
the restaurants right now

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:49.660
for the last two years

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:51.909
it's been the roughest
time ever for our industry.

00:04:51.909 --> 00:04:52.742
So we really need it.

00:04:52.742 --> 00:04:53.740
- Because of being around it, yeah.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:54.600
You weren't allowed to be, yeah.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.240
It's, it's been really
crazy and I was a waitress.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.800
So like that's literally your
livelihood is getting tips

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:01.650
and working off of that.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:05.210
So well, Curtis, you are
active with No Kid Hungry.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:06.358
And why is this important to you?

00:05:06.358 --> 00:05:08.520
- Look, No Kid Hungry makes sure that we,

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.830
we have a ton of food
insecurity in America.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.158
And when you think about
a food insecure child

00:05:13.158 --> 00:05:15.688
it's a horrible, horrible
thing to think about.

00:05:15.688 --> 00:05:16.521
- (Kelly) Yeah.

00:05:16.521 --> 00:05:17.780
- So what No Kid Hungry attempts to do

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:19.440
is make sure that kids get food.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:20.620
It's that simple, you know,

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:21.915
and whether that's in their classrooms,

00:05:21.915 --> 00:05:23.240
first thing in the morning,

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:24.555
because there wasn't breakfast at home,

00:05:24.555 --> 00:05:27.180
or whether that's making
sure that kids get access

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.490
to pantry items for their houses.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.800
So look, I think all of us,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.690
whether it's your number
one charity or not,

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.467
we all support No Kid Hungry because

00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:36.300
- (Kelly) Absolutely.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.310
- It's, it's just such
a wonderful, wonderful.

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:39.450
So that's another way that you can

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:43.410
support people in need is
to go to No Kid Hungry.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:44.590
- Especially cause those kids that's,

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:45.730
they're not in that position

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:47.562
because of anything they've done.

00:05:47.562 --> 00:05:48.395
- [Curtis] Right.

00:05:48.395 --> 00:05:49.228
- You know? They just landed there.

00:05:49.228 --> 00:05:50.210
Those are the cards that were dealt.

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:51.870
So, and especially the last two years,

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.120
Because kids were out of school too.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:54.630
Like, a lot of those people

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:56.811
that survived on those meals
at school didn't have them.

00:05:56.811 --> 00:05:57.644
- [Marcus] Exactly.

00:05:57.644 --> 00:05:59.110
- And everybody was kind of food poor

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:00.330
because of the pandemic

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:01.750
and everything was scarce in places.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:03.194
It was really scary.

00:06:03.194 --> 00:06:05.620
It's very cool though that
you take your spotlight

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:07.059
and do something positive for others.

00:06:07.059 --> 00:06:07.892
(audience applauds)

00:06:07.892 --> 00:06:10.630
Like, you know, you could
just succeed yourself

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:11.510
and be on your merry way,

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:13.038
but it's cool that you give that.

00:06:13.038 --> 00:06:13.871
- It's cool,

00:06:13.871 --> 00:06:15.818
and I get to meet these
beautiful little kids, you know?

00:06:15.818 --> 00:06:16.651
- (Kelly) Yeah.

00:06:16.651 --> 00:06:18.100
- I go to these schools
and I cook them breakfast

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.681
and it's really fulfilling.

00:06:19.681 --> 00:06:20.980
- And you have no idea how many
you even inspire to do that.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:21.813
But you do.

00:06:21.813 --> 00:06:22.770
- Yeah. That's fun.
- Yeah.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.990
I wish I'd have had
anyone cooking around me.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.546
Maybe that would've helped, Curtis.

00:06:26.546 --> 00:06:27.713
I don't know.

